<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philopoemen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 253–182 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Philopoemen of Megalopolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philopoemen was a general and statesman of Megalopolis who served eight times as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> of the Achaean League. He reformed the league's army on the Macedonian model, defeated the Spartan tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machanidas">Machanidas</a> at Mantinea in 207 BCE and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> into the league in 192 BCE. Captured by the rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messenians">Messenians</a> in 182 BCE, he was forced to drink poison.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philopoemen.jpg" alt="Philopoemen" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapofphilopoemen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philopoemen was born at Megalopolis, the son of Craugis, whose death left him to be adopted by an important citizen of the city, Cleander. His teachers were the academic philosophers Ecdemus and Demophanes, Megapolitans who had helped to depose tyrants, and he absorbed ideas of freedom and democracy.</p><p>He strove to emulate the Theban general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> and held that a public servant must be personally virtuous, so he wore humble clothes all his life and spurned expensive adornment. He first came to notice in 223 BCE when he helped defend Megalopolis against the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a>, losing his horse and being wounded but fighting on until the citizens had time to evacuate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia">Battle of Sellasia</a>, Philopoemen led a cavalry force that attacked when the Illyrian infantry were surrounded, and he fought on behind enemy lines after a javelin wound. The Spartans were massacred and Cleomenes fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. From 221 BCE he spent ten years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> captain. Returning in 210 BCE, he became cavalry commander of the Achaean League and killed Damophantus, who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> and Eleans, with a lance thrust.</p><p>Appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> in 209 BCE, he modernised and enlarged the Achaean army, updating its equipment and tactics. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-207-bce">Battle of Mantinea</a> in 207 BCE the Achaeans defeated the Spartans, and Philopoemen killed their ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machanidas">Machanidas</a> in single combat. The Achaeans set up a bronze statue of the fight at Delphi.</p><p>He later drove the successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabis">Nabis</a> from Messene, and after a second spell as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> leader in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, of six years, was again <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> from 193 BCE to fight Nabis. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> murdered Nabis and briefly held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, Philopoemen entered the city with his army and forced Sparta into the League. In 188 BCE his army demolished Nabis's wall, he restored the exiles, abolished Spartan law and education and introduced Achaean institutions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>These measures provoked opposition even among his supporters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, whose opponents appealed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a>. Philopoemen and his supporters rejected every Roman proposal, arguing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had recognised the League's independence by treaty, and Achaean politics split. When Messene revolted under Dinocrates, Philopoemen marched against them, was thrown from his horse behind enemy lines, captured and invited to drink poison. The League's members then joined forces and took Messene. His body was cremated and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> carried the urn at the public funeral, later writing his biography. Pausanias wrote that after his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> ceased to bear good men.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philopoemen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army was transformed by <b>Philopoemen of Megalopolis</b>, who as hipparch and then, from 208 BCE, as general retrained the cavalry, re-equipped the infantry as a Macedonian-style phalanx with long pikes and larger shields, and encouraged a martial pride among the citizens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the rule of Nabis, <b>Philopoemen</b> forced Sparta into the Achaean League in 192 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Philopoemen</b> moved the meetings in rotation among member cities in the 180s BCE, reducing Aegium's role.</blockquote><a href="/state/aegium">Aegium</a></li><li><blockquote>Cleomenes III of Sparta sacked it in 223 BCE, but it was rebuilt and produced the Achaean leader <b>Philopoemen</b> and the historian Polybius.</blockquote><a href="/state/megalopolis">Megalopolis</a></li><li><blockquote>Messene joined the Achaean League in 191 BCE, revolted, and in 182 BCE executed the captured Achaean general <b>Philopoemen</b> before being forced back.</blockquote><a href="/state/messene">Messene</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Megalopolis, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Messene, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.41,22.13&amp;year=-207" aria-label="Megalopolis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5103 817 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philopoemen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5553 1087h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5553 1087h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.41,22.13&amp;year=-207">What was here in 207 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
